## Algorithm

```text
Initialize:
    left = 0
    maxLength = 0
    frequency map

For right = 0 to n - 1:

    Increase frequency of nums[right]

    While frequency of nums[right] > k:
        Decrease frequency of nums[left]
        Move left forward

    Update maximum length:
        maxLength = max(maxLength, right - left + 1)

Return maxLength
```

---

## Complexity Analysis

Let `n` be the length of `nums`.

### Time Complexity

```text
O(n)
```

Each element is added to the window once and removed from the window at most once.

### Space Complexity

```text
O(n)
```

In the worst case, the frequency map can contain all distinct elements.

---

## C++ Solution

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int maxSubarrayLength(vector<int>& nums, int k) {
        int n = nums.size();
        int maxlen = 0;

        unordered_map<int, int> freq;

        for (int i = 0, j = 0; j < n; j++) {

            // Increase frequency of current element
            int &count = freq[nums[j]];
            count++;

            // Shrink window while frequency exceeds k
            while (count > k) {
                freq[nums[i++]]--;
            }

            // Update maximum valid window length
            maxlen = max(maxlen, j - i + 1);
        }

        return maxlen;
    }
};
```

## Key Insight

The core idea is to maintain a **valid sliding window** where every element occurs at most `k` times.

```text
Expand right
     ↓
Frequency exceeds k?
     ↓
   Yes
     ↓
Move left until valid
     ↓
Update maximum length
```

This avoids checking every possible subarray and reduces the solution from a potentially `O(n²)` approach to **O(n)**.
